nodes.statement:
A local chart for $X$ is a diagram:
\begin{equation} \begin{tikzcd}
X\ar[hookleftarrow]{r}{\text{open}}&s_\alpha^{-1}(0)\ar[hook]{r}{\text{closed}}&X_\alpha\ar{r}{s_\alpha}&E_\alpha
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $E_\alpha$ is a finite-dimensional vector space (called the \emph{obstruction space}), $X_\alpha$ is an auxiliary moduli space (called the \emph{$\alpha$-thickened\footnote{Perhaps a better name would be ``$\alpha$-stabilized moduli space'', though we have decided not to risk confusing this notion of stabilization (i.e.\ product with a vector space) and the notion of stabilizing a Riemann surface (i.e.\ adding marked points).} moduli space}), and $s_\alpha$ is called the \emph{Kuranishi map}.

It comes with a canonical isomorphism:\footnote{In the present discussion, we ignore issues about orientations.}
\begin{equation} \check H^\bullet(X)\xrightarrow\sim H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(X;A)
\end{equation}
($H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}$ is the homology of $C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}$) and with a canonical map:
\begin{equation} C^{d+\bullet}_{\mathrm{vir}}(X;A)\xrightarrow{s_\ast}C_{\dim E_A-\bullet}(E_A,E_A\setminus 0)
\end{equation}
(where $E_A:=\bigoplus_{\alpha\in A}E_\alpha$ and $d$ is the virtual dimension of $A$).

First, let us imagine we have a single chart \eqref{localchart} which is global (i.e.\ $X=s_\alpha^{-1}(0)$) and $s_\alpha^{-1}(0)\subseteq X_\alpha^{\mathrm{reg}}$.  Then we consider the following diagram:
\begin{equation}
\check H^{\dim X_\alpha^{\mathrm{reg}}-\dim E_\alpha}(X)\xrightarrow\sim H_{\dim E_\alpha}(X_\alpha^{\mathrm{reg}},X_\alpha^{\mathrm{reg}}\setminus X)\xrightarrow{(s_\alpha)_\ast}H_{\dim E_\alpha}(E_\alpha,E_\alpha\setminus 0)=\mathbb Z
\end{equation}
The first isomorphism is Poincar\'e--Lefschetz duality.  The virtual fundamental class is the resulting element $[X]^{\mathrm{vir}}\in\check H^d(X)^\vee$ (recall $d=\dim X_\alpha^{\mathrm{reg}}-\dim E_\alpha$) which is easily seen to agree with the class defined via perturbation.  Thus in this case, the complex $C_{\dim X_\alpha^{\mathrm{reg}}-\bullet}(X_\alpha^{\mathrm{reg}},X_\alpha^{\mathrm{reg}}\setminus X)$ plays the role of $C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(X;A)$.

Then for any finite subset $I=\{\alpha_1,\ldots,\alpha_n\}\subseteq A$, there is an ``overlap chart'':
\begin{align} X_I&:=X_{\alpha_1}\underset X\times\cdots\underset X\times X_{\alpha_n}\\
\Gamma_I&:=\Gamma_{\alpha_1}\times\cdots\times\Gamma_{\alpha_n}
\end{align}
(where \eqref{fpoverorbifold} is the ``orbifold fiber product''; see Remark \ref{orbifoldfiberproductrmk} below).

It is easy to check that $X_I$ is a smooth manifold with a smooth action by $\Gamma_I$ and that:
\begin{equation}
X_I/\Gamma_I=V_{\alpha_1}\cap\cdots\cap V_{\alpha_n}\subseteq X
\end{equation}

Now our thickenings are:
\begin{equation} X_I:=\Bigl\{(x,\{e_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in I})\in\bigcap_{\alpha\in I}V_\alpha\times\bigoplus_{\alpha\in I}E_\alpha\Bigm|s(x)+\sum_{\alpha\in I}\lambda_\alpha(x,e_\alpha)=0\Bigr\}
\end{equation}

Now our thickening $\overline{\mathcal M}_{0,0}(X,B)_I$ (any finite $I\subseteq A$) is defined as the set of:
\begin{rlist}
\item A smooth map $u:C\to X$ where $C$ is a nodal curve of genus $0$ so that for all $\alpha\in I$, we have $u\pitchfork D_\alpha$ (meaning $u^{-1}(\partial D_\alpha)=\varnothing$ and for all $p\in u^{-1}(D_\alpha)$, the differential $(du)_p:T_pC\to T_{u(p)}X/T_{u(p)}D_\alpha$ is surjective and $p$ is not a node of $C$) with $\#u^{-1}(D_\alpha)=r_\alpha$ such that adding these $r_\alpha$ intersections as marked points makes $C$ stable.
\item Elements $e_\alpha\in E_\alpha$ for all $\alpha\in I$
\item Labellings of $u^{-1}(D_\alpha)$ by $\{1,\ldots,r_\alpha\}$ for all $\alpha\in I$.
\end{rlist}
such that:
\begin{equation} \bar\partial u+\sum_{\alpha\in I}\lambda_\alpha(e_\alpha)(\phi_\alpha,u)=0
\end{equation}
where $\phi_\alpha:C\to\overline{\mathcal C}_{0,r_\alpha}$ is the unique isomorphism onto a fiber respecting the labeling of $u^{-1}(D_\alpha)$.

As a third try (which ends up working nicely), let us consider the following ``deformation to the normal cone'':
\begin{equation}
Y_{\alpha\beta}:=\left\{(e_\alpha,e_\beta,t,x)\in E_\alpha\times E_\beta\times[0,1]\times X_{\alpha\beta}\;\middle|\;\begin{matrix}s_\alpha(x)=t\cdot e_\alpha\hfill\cr s_\beta(x)=(1-t)\cdot e_\beta\hfill\end{matrix}\right\}
\end{equation}

Now we consider the mapping cone of the following:
\begin{equation} \begin{matrix}
C_{\dim X_{\alpha\beta}-\bullet}(U_{\alpha,\alpha\beta}\times E_\beta,U_{\alpha,\alpha\beta}\times E_\beta\setminus U_\alpha\times 0)\cr\oplus\cr
C_{\dim X_{\alpha\beta}-\bullet}(E_\alpha\times U_{\beta,\alpha\beta},E_\alpha\times U_{\beta,\alpha\beta}\setminus 0\times U_\beta)
\end{matrix}
\to
\begin{matrix}
C_{\dim X_{\alpha\beta}-\bullet}(X_\alpha\times E_\beta,X_\alpha\times E_\beta\setminus U_\alpha\times 0)\cr\oplus\cr
C_{\dim X_{\alpha\beta}-\bullet}(Y_{\alpha\beta},Y_{\alpha\beta}\setminus 0\times[0,1]\times U_{\alpha\beta})\cr\oplus\cr
C_{\dim X_{\alpha\beta}-\bullet}(E_\alpha\times X_\beta,E_\alpha\times X_\beta\setminus 0\times U_\beta)
\end{matrix}
\end{equation}
The maps are simply pushforward along the maps of spaces (with appropriate signs):
\begin{align}
U_{\alpha,\alpha\beta}\times E_\beta&\hookrightarrow X_\alpha\times E_\beta\\
U_{\alpha,\alpha\beta}\times E_\beta&\hookrightarrow Y_{\alpha\beta}\quad\text{(isomorphism onto $t=1$ fiber)}\\
E_\alpha\times U_{\beta,\alpha\beta}&\hookrightarrow Y_{\alpha\beta}\quad\text{(isomorphism onto $t=0$ fiber)}\\
E_\alpha\times U_{\beta,\alpha\beta}&\hookrightarrow E_\alpha\times X_\beta
\end{align}

Now $\mathcal F^\bullet$ satisfies the following key properties:
\begin{rlist}
\item(``$\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf'') The total complex of the following double complex is acyclic:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal F^\bullet(K_1\cup K_2)\to\mathcal F^\bullet(K_1)\oplus\mathcal F^\bullet(K_2)\to\mathcal F^\bullet(K_1\cap K_2)
\end{equation}
This is essentially just a restatement of the Mayer--Vietoris exact sequence.
\item(``$\mathcal F^\bullet$ is pure and $H^0\mathcal F^\bullet=\mathfrak o_M$'') The homology of $\mathcal F^\bullet(\{p\})$ (namely $H_{n-\bullet}(M,M\setminus p)$) is concentrated in degree zero, where it can be canonically identified with the fiber of $\mathfrak o_M$ (the orientation sheaf of $M$) at $p\in M$.
\end{rlist}
(the precise notions of a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf and of purity are given in Definitions \ref{homotopyKsheaf} and \ref{purehomotopyKsheaf}; in the above statements they have been simplified for sake of exposition).  A formal consequence (Proposition \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}) of the fact that $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf with $H^0\mathcal F^\bullet=\mathfrak o_M$ is that there is a canonical isomorphism:
\begin{equation} H_{n-i}(M,M\setminus K)=H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K)=\check H^i(K;\mathfrak o_M)
\end{equation}
Specializing to $K=M$, we have derived the Poincar\'e duality isomorphism $H_{n-i}(M)=\check H^i(M;\mathfrak o_M)$.

To define $d$, we need to choose:\footnote{That these should be contractible choices is suggested by the Dold--Thom--Almgren theorem \cite{almgren} \cite[p430]{gromovI}.}
\begin{rlist}
\item(``Chain level coherent orientations'') Cochains $\lambda(p,q)\in C^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X(p,q)\operatorname{rel}\partial)$ satisfying the following property.  Define:
\begin{equation} \mu(p,r):=\sum_{p\prec q\prec r}\lambda(p,q)\cdot\lambda(q,r)\in C_{\mathrm{vir}}^0(\partial\mathcal X(p,r))
\end{equation}
(where we implicitly use \eqref{productmapfromIA} on the right hand side).  We require that $d\lambda(p,r)$ equal (the image under \eqref{introcoboundary}) of $\mu(p,r)$.  Thus $(\mu(p,r),\lambda(p,r))$ defines a cycle in the mapping cone of \eqref{introcoboundary}, and thus a homology class in $H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X(p,r))=\check H^0(\mathcal X(p,r),\mathfrak o_{\mathcal X(p,r)})$ (by \eqref{HXisointro}).  We require that this homology class coincide with the (given) orientation on $\mathcal X(p,r)$.
\item(``Thom cocycles'') Cocycles $[[E_{\mathcal A(p,q)}]]\in C^{\dim E_{\mathcal A(p,q)}}(E_{\mathcal A(p,q)},E_{\mathcal A(p,q)}\setminus 0)$ whose pairing with $[E_{\mathcal A(p,q)}]$ is $1$ and which are compatible in the sense that the following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation} \hspace{-1.5cm}
\begin{tikzcd}
C_\bullet(E_{\mathcal A(p,q)},E_{\mathcal A(p,q)}\setminus 0)\otimes C_\bullet(E_{\mathcal A(q,r)},E_{\mathcal A(q,r)}\setminus 0)\ar{r}\ar{d}[swap]{[[E_{\mathcal A(p,q)}]]\otimes[[E_{\mathcal A(q,r)}]]}&C_\bullet(E_{\mathcal A(p,r)},E_{\mathcal A(p,r)}\setminus 0)\ar{ld}{[[E_{\mathcal A(p,r)}]]}\\
\mathbb Z
\end{tikzcd}
\hspace{-1.5cm}
\end{equation}
\end{rlist}

Let us now sketch the proof of the existence of $\lambda$ and $[[E]]$ as above.  We will work by induction on $(p,q)$; that is, we show that valid $\lambda(p,q)$ and $[[E_{\mathcal A(p,q)}]]$ exist given that we have fixed valid $\lambda(p',q')$ and $[[E_{\mathcal A(p',q')}]]$ for all $p\preceq p'\prec q'\preceq q$ (other than $(p',q')=(p,q)$).

To construct $\lambda(p,q)$, argue as follows.  Notice that $\mu(p,q)$ is automatically a cycle (apply \eqref{boundarychainlevelcoherent} to expand its boundary and everything cancels).  Now from a general statement about cycles in mapping cones, the existence of $\lambda(p,q)$ inducing the correct homology class in $H_{\mathrm{vir}}^0(\mathcal X(p,q))=\check H^0(\mathcal X(p,q);\mathfrak o_{\mathcal X(p,q)})$ reduces to showing that the homology class $[\mu(p,q)]\in H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(\partial\mathcal X(p,q))=\check H^0(\partial\mathcal X(p,q),\mathfrak o_{\partial\mathcal X(p,q)})$ is correct (namely, that it coincides with the image of the desired class under the coboundary map $\check H^0(\mathcal X(p,q);\mathfrak o_{\mathcal X(p,q)})\to\check H^0(\partial\mathcal X(p,q),\mathfrak o_{\partial\mathcal X(p,q)})$).  The key observation is that \emph{this can be checked locally} since $\mathfrak o_{\partial\mathcal X(p,q)}$ is a sheaf.  Over the top strata of $\partial\mathcal X(p,q)$ (that is, those trajectories that split only once), the agreement is clear by the induction hypothesis on $\lambda$ and the compatibility of the given coherent orientations.  Unfortunately, the top strata may not be dense in $\partial\mathcal X(p,q)$, so we need to work harder (see Proposition \ref{stratifiedispurehomotopysheaf}).  In the end, we must use the induction hypothesis for \emph{all} $\lambda(p',q')$.

To construct $[[E_{\mathcal A(p,r)}]]$, argue as follows.  First, observe that the ``homology diagram'' trivially commutes:
\begin{equation} \begin{tikzcd}
H_\bullet(E_{\mathcal A(p,q)},E_{\mathcal A(p,q)}\setminus 0)\otimes H_\bullet(E_{\mathcal A(q,r)},E_{\mathcal A(q,r)}\setminus 0)\ar{r}\ar{d}[swap]{[E_{\mathcal A(p,q)}]\otimes[E_{\mathcal A(q,r)}]\mapsto 1}&H_\bullet(E_{\mathcal A(p,r)},E_{\mathcal A(p,r)}\setminus 0)\ar{ld}{[E_{\mathcal A(p,r)}]\mapsto 1}\\
\mathbb Z
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
If the horizontal map in \eqref{coherentcofundamentalcocycles} were a cofibration (think ``injective'') in a suitable sense, then the commutativity of \eqref{coherentcofundamentalcocycleshomology} would be sufficient to imply the existence of $[[E_{\mathcal A(p,r)}]]$.  Unfortunately, it is far from clear that this map is a cofibration; moreover, its failure to be a cofibration is a genuine obstruction to defining $[[E_{\mathcal A(p,r)}]]$ inductively.  For the correct inductive construction, we must use ``cofibrant replacements'' for the system of complexes $C_\bullet(E_{\mathcal A(p,q)},E_{\mathcal A(p,q)}\setminus 0)$ (the details of which we leave for \S\ref{homologygroupssection}).

Let us now prove our assertion $\pi_\ast[X]^{\mathrm{vir}}=0$ in the simple case of a single chart (for which we defined $[X]^{\mathrm{vir}}$ in \S\ref{vfcsinglechart}).  In this case, the implicit atlas $A=\{\alpha\}$ consists of a topological manifold $X_\alpha$, a function $s_\alpha:X_\alpha\to E_\alpha$, and an identification $X=s_\alpha^{-1}(0)$.  This atlas being $S^1$-equivariant means that $X_\alpha$ is equipped with an $S^1$-action for which $s_\alpha$ is $S^1$-invariant and which induces the given action on $X$.  The desired statement follows from the following diagram, as we explain below:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{CD}
\check H^d(X/S^1)@=H_{\dim E_\alpha-1}^{S^1}(X_\alpha,X_\alpha\setminus X)@>(s_\alpha)_\ast>>H_{\dim E_\alpha-1}^{S^1}(E_\alpha,E_\alpha\setminus 0)\cr
@V\pi^\ast VV@VV\pi^!V@VV\pi^!V\cr
\check H^d(X)@=H_{\dim E_\alpha}(X_\alpha,X_\alpha\setminus X)@>(s_\alpha)_\ast>>H_{\dim E_\alpha}(E_\alpha,E_\alpha\setminus 0)@>[E_\alpha]\mapsto 1>>\mathbb Z
\end{CD}
\end{equation*}
The map $\pi^\ast$ is pullback under $\pi:X\to X/S^1$.  The maps $\pi^!$ are the maps from the Gysin long exact sequence for an $S^1$-space:
\begin{equation}
\cdots\xrightarrow{\cap e}H_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(Z)\xrightarrow{\pi^!}H_\bullet(Z)\xrightarrow{\pi_\ast}H_\bullet^{S^1}(Z)\xrightarrow{\cap e}H_{\bullet-2}^{S^1}(Z)\xrightarrow{\pi^!}\cdots
\end{equation}
The two leftmost horizontal identifications are a form of Poincar\'e duality (which can be proven, along with the commutativity of the square, with homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves as in \S\ref{homotopysheavesintrosec}); it is at this step where we use the fact that $S^1$ acts freely on $X$.

Now $[X]^{\mathrm{vir}}$ is by definition the composition of the bottom row.  Hence $\pi_\ast[X]^{\mathrm{vir}}$ is the map from the upper left corner to the bottom right.  On the other hand, the rightmost vertical map $\pi^!:H_{\dim E_\alpha-1}^{S^1}(E_\alpha,E_\alpha\setminus 0)\to H_{\dim E_\alpha}(E_\alpha,E_\alpha\setminus 0)$ vanishes since $S^1$ acts trivially on $E_\alpha$.  It follows that $\pi_\ast[X]^{\mathrm{vir}}=0$ as desired.

The desired statement follows from the following diagram, as we explain below:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{CD}
\check H^d(X/S^1)@=H_{\dim E_\alpha-1}^{S^1}(X_\alpha,X_\alpha\setminus X)@>(s_\alpha)_\ast>>H_{\dim E_\alpha-1}^{S^1}(E_\alpha,E_\alpha\setminus 0)\cr
@V\pi^\ast VV@VV\pi^!V@VV\pi^!V\cr
\check H^d(X)@=H_{\dim E_\alpha}(X_\alpha,X_\alpha\setminus X)@>(s_\alpha)_\ast>>H_{\dim E_\alpha}(E_\alpha,E_\alpha\setminus 0)@>[E_\alpha]\mapsto 1>>\mathbb Z
\end{CD}
\end{equation*}

The map $\pi^\ast$ is pullback under $\pi:X\to X/S^1$.  The maps $\pi^!$ are the maps from the Gysin long exact sequence for an $S^1$-space:
\begin{equation}
\cdots\xrightarrow{\cap e}H_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(Z)\xrightarrow{\pi^!}H_\bullet(Z)\xrightarrow{\pi_\ast}H_\bullet^{S^1}(Z)\xrightarrow{\cap e}H_{\bullet-2}^{S^1}(Z)\xrightarrow{\pi^!}\cdots
\end{equation}

There is a canonical map:
\begin{equation} C^{d+\bullet}_{\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\xrightarrow{s_\ast}C_{\dim E_A-\bullet}(E_A,E_A\setminus 0;\mathfrak o_{E_A}^\vee)^{\Gamma_A}
\end{equation}
which can be thought of as the (chain level) \emph{virtual fundamental cycle}.

To study the virtual cochain complexes (in particular, to construct the isomorphisms \eqref{HXiso}--\eqref{HXreliso}), we define complexes of $\mathcal K$-presheaves on $X$:
\begin{align}
K&\mapsto C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)\\
K&\mapsto C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)
\end{align}
whose global sections are the virtual cochain complexes.\footnote{Note that this is a certain abuse of notation, as $A$ is not an implicit atlas on $K\subsetneqq X$.}\footnote{Note that the map $s_\ast$ \eqref{sastintrotvfcsec} is global; it does not exist on $C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)$ for $K\subsetneqq X$ or on any $C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)$.}  We show that they are pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves, and that there are canonical isomorphisms of sheaves on $X$:
\begin{align}
H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)&=\mathfrak o_X\\
H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)&=\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}
\end{align}
The isomorphisms \eqref{HXiso}--\eqref{HXreliso} then follow from Proposition \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}.

Hence we may apply Poincar\'e--Lefschetz duality in the form of Lemma \ref{poincareduality} to see that:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\check H_c^{\bullet+\#I}(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J)\times\mathbb R_{>0}^I,\mathfrak o_{\mathbb R}^{\otimes I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee\otimes\mathfrak o_{X_J^{\mathrm{reg}}\operatorname{rel}\partial})^{\Gamma_A}\\
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)_{IJ}&=\check H_c^{\bullet+\#I}(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J)\times\mathbb R_{>0}^I,\mathfrak o_{\mathbb R}^{\otimes I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee\otimes\mathfrak o_{X_J^{\mathrm{reg}}})^{\Gamma_A}
\end{align}
(the orientation sheaf of $X_{I,J,A}$ is given by $\mathfrak o_{X_J^{\mathrm{reg}}}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_A}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee\otimes\mathfrak o_{\mathbb R}^{\otimes I}$).

Now we apply the K\"unneth formula to conclude that:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J),\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee\otimes\mathfrak o_{X_J^{\mathrm{reg}}\operatorname{rel}\partial})^{\Gamma_A}\\
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J),\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee\otimes\mathfrak o_{X_J^{\mathrm{reg}}})^{\Gamma_A}
\end{align}

Since $X$ is locally orientable, we have $\mathfrak o_{X_J^{\mathrm{reg}}}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee=\psi_{\varnothing J}^\ast\mathfrak o_X$, so we can write:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J),\psi_{\varnothing J}^\ast\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})^{\Gamma_A}\\
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J),\psi_{\varnothing J}^\ast\mathfrak o_X)^{\Gamma_A}
\end{align}

Now $\Gamma_A$ acts through the projection $\Gamma_A\to\Gamma_J$, and $\psi_{\varnothing J}:\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J)\to K\cap V_I\cap V_J$ is exactly the quotient by $\Gamma_J$.  Hence by Lemma \ref{cechcohomologyfinitequotient} we get isomorphisms:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(K\cap V_I\cap V_J,\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\\
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(K\cap V_I\cap V_J,\mathfrak o_X)
\end{align}

Now use Lemma \ref{cptcechembeddingproperty} to write this as:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet(K,j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\\
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet(K,j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_X)
\end{align}

By the definition of the vertical identifications, the commutativity of the first two squares of \eqref{compatibilityoflesdiagram} reduces to the commutativity of the diagram of sheaves:
\begin{equation} \begin{tikzcd}
H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofF}}\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{Cpure}}}&H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofF}}\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{Cpure}}}&H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{Cpure}}}\\
\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofO}}&\mathfrak o_X\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofO}}&i_\ast\mathfrak o_{\partial X}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}

In fact, the commutativity of the last square of \eqref{compatibilityoflesdiagram} is also a consequence of the commutativity of \eqref{sheafcommutativity}.  To see this, consider the following diagram:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = small]
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(\partial X;A)\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}}}&\ar{l}[swap]{\sim}H^\bullet[C^{\bullet+1}_{\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\to C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(X;A)]\ar{r}\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}}}&H^{\bullet+1}_{\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}}}\\
\check H^\bullet(\partial X;H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(-;A))\ar{d}{\sim}&\ar{l}[swap]{\sim}\check H^\bullet(X;[H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)[1]\to H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)])\ar{r}\ar{d}{\sim}&\check H^{\bullet+1}(X,H^0_{\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A))\ar{d}{\sim}\\
\check H^\bullet(\partial X;\mathfrak o_{\partial X})&\ar{l}[swap]{\sim}\check H^\bullet(X;[\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}[1]\to\mathfrak o_X])\ar{r}&\check H^{\bullet+1}(X,\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}

Hence it suffices to show that the following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A')\ar{d}\ar[equal]{r}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\ar[equal]{d}\\
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar[equal]{r}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where the left vertical arrow is induced by the obvious pushforward on chains.

We may assume that $A$ is finite.  There is a natural isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(X\sqcup Y\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)=C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\oplus C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(Y\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)
\end{equation}
compatible with the map to $C_{-\bullet}(E;A)$.

Thus it suffices to show that the following square of isomorphisms commutes:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(X\sqcup Y\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar[equal]{d}\ar[equal]{r}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&\check H^\bullet(X\sqcup Y;\mathfrak o_{X\sqcup Y\operatorname{rel}\partial})\ar[equal]{d}\\
H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\oplus H^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(Y\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar[equal]{r}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\oplus\check H^\bullet(Y;\mathfrak o_{Y\operatorname{rel}\partial})
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
By definition of the horizontal maps, it suffices to check the commutativity of the corresponding diagram of sheaves on $X\sqcup Y$, which is clear.

We may replace $A$ with the finite subatlas $\varnothing\subseteq A$.  Now in this case, we have:
\begin{equation}
C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;\varnothing)=C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;\varnothing)_{\varnothing\varnothing}=C_{d-\bullet}(X_{\varnothing,\varnothing,\varnothing},X_{\varnothing,\varnothing,\varnothing}\setminus X_{\varnothing,\varnothing,\varnothing}^K)=C_{d-\bullet}(X,X\setminus K)
\end{equation}

Now $\partial\tilde X=X\sqcup X'$, so we have $[X]^{\mathrm{vir}}=[X']^{\mathrm{vir}}$ as homology classes on $\tilde X$ (Lemmas \ref{disjointunionfc} and \ref{cobordismworks}).  Hence we have:
\begin{equation}
[X]^{\mathrm{vir}}=[X']^{\mathrm{vir}}
\end{equation}
as elements of $H^{\dim B-\dim E}(B;\mathfrak o_B\otimes\mathfrak o_E^\vee)^\vee$.

Let $(M,\partial M)	o(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$ be cell-like. Then:  egin{rlist} \itemIf $\mathfrak s\prec\mathfrak t$ then $M^{\mathfrak s}\subseteq\partial M^{\leq\mathfrak t}$. \itemIf $M^{\mathfrak s}
